      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Choosing between a metal roof and asphalt shingles in Utah comes down to balancing your upfront budget, how long you plan to stay in the home, and how well the material handles Utah's heavy winter snow, high-altitude UV rays, and extreme temperature shifts.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "balancing your upfront budget, how long you plan to stay in the home, and how well the material handles Utah's heavy winter snow, high-altitude UV rays, and extreme temperature shifts"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Cost Comparison"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Asphalt Shingles:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Installed Price: About $3.00 to $8.00 per square foot ($8,000 to $16,000 for an average 2,000 sq. ft. home)."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Lifespan: 20 to 25 years.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  0,
                  2
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Metal Roof (Standing Seam):",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Installed Price: About $8.00 to $18.00 per square foot ($16,000 to $36,000+ for an average 2,000 sq. ft. home)."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Lifespan: 40 to 70+ years.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  0,
                  2
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              2
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Performance in Utah's Climate"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Snow and Ice"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Metal Roof: The slick surface and standing seams naturally and rapidly shed heavy snow, preventing dangerous accumulation and reducing ice dam risks at the eaves (though snow guards are recommended if you have walkways or entryways below).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Asphalt Shingles: Shingles grip snow longer. While this stops sudden snow slides, the freeze-thaw cycles common in Utah winters can lead to ice dams and stress the shingle edges.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Wind and Storms"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Metal Roof: Interlocking standing-seam panels have no exposed fasteners or edges for high alpine gusts to catch, easily enduring wind speeds up to 140 mph.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Asphalt Shingles: Quality architectural shingles (like a Class 4 impact-rated or 130-mph rated shingle) perform well, but high winds, granule loss, and age can cause shingles to lift or \"unzip\" over time.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              6
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Sun and Heat (UV Exposure)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Metal Roof: Reflects high-altitude solar radiation, keeping attic spaces cooler and lowering summer cooling bills (especially with cool-roof coatings).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Asphalt Shingles: Absorbs more solar heat and can dry out, crack, or curl faster under intense dry mountain UV exposure.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              8
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
